1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,376
Every person and every service on a Linux server runs as a user.

2
00:00:04,396 --> 00:00:07,935
Create the wrong account, or forget to delete an old one,

3
00:00:07,955 --> 00:00:09,745
and you have a security hole.

4
00:00:10,215 --> 00:00:14,334
In this video: create, delete, and modify local user accounts.

5
00:00:18,854 --> 00:00:21,437
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:21,907 --> 00:00:25,087
Create, delete, and modify local user accounts.

7
00:00:25,557 --> 00:00:29,602
We'll create a user, create one with options, change an existing user,

8
00:00:29,622 --> 00:00:32,854
and delete users, with and without their home directory.

9
00:00:40,374 --> 00:00:43,949
Every local user is one line in the file /etc/passwd.

10
00:00:43,969 --> 00:00:49,037
You see a line with getent passwd. It has seven parts, separated by colons:

11
00:00:49,057 --> 00:00:53,854
the name, a password placeholder, the user ID, the group ID, a comment,

12
00:00:53,874 --> 00:00:56,567
the home directory, and the login shell.

13
00:01:06,037 --> 00:01:11,634
Three commands do the job. useradd creates. usermod modifies. userdel deletes.

14
00:01:11,654 --> 00:01:16,243
We use sudo, because only the administrator can change accounts.

15
00:01:16,263 --> 00:01:19,628
In this demo, the lab changes nothing silently.

16
00:01:27,148 --> 00:01:31,966
Create a user: sudo useradd alice. No output means it worked.

17
00:01:31,986 --> 00:01:36,487
The command id shows her user ID and group ID, both 1001.

18
00:01:36,507 --> 00:01:42,197
getent passwd alice shows her line, with the home directory /home/alice.

19
00:01:42,217 --> 00:01:47,352
And ls -d lists that directory itself: useradd created it for us.

20
00:02:00,872 --> 00:02:05,235
Options let you decide the details. -u sets the user ID.

21
00:02:05,255 --> 00:02:10,556
-c sets the comment, usually the full name. -s sets the login shell.

22
00:02:10,576 --> 00:02:15,956
The line shows user ID 1500, the comment Bob Lee, and the shell bash.

23
00:02:26,476 --> 00:02:31,633
To change an existing user, use usermod, with the same options.

24
00:02:31,653 --> 00:02:36,809
-s with /usr/sbin/nologin means this user can no longer log in.

25
00:02:36,829 --> 00:02:41,411
-c changes the comment. The line now shows both changes.

26
00:02:50,931 --> 00:02:56,295
To delete a user: userdel bob. getent now finds nothing: the account is gone.

27
00:02:56,315 --> 00:03:01,748
But ls shows the home directory is still there. userdel alone keeps the files.

28
00:03:10,268 --> 00:03:13,341
To remove the home directory too, add -r.

29
00:03:13,361 --> 00:03:18,019
We create carol, then delete her with userdel -r. Now ls says:

30
00:03:18,039 --> 00:03:21,640
no such file or directory. The home is gone too.

31
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:34,596
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

32
00:03:36,116 --> 00:03:40,523
We reboot. Alice is still there, with her changes. Bob is still gone.

33
00:03:40,543 --> 00:03:44,501
Account changes are saved in a file, so they survive a reboot.

34
00:03:52,021 --> 00:03:57,111
Your turn. Create the user sam, with user ID 3001 and the comment Sam Smith.

35
00:03:57,131 --> 00:03:59,464
Change the shell of sam to nologin.

36
00:03:59,484 --> 00:04:02,893
Delete the user temp, including the home directory.

37
00:04:02,913 --> 00:04:07,532
The lab silently creates temp first, so you have something to delete.

38
00:04:07,552 --> 00:04:10,625
Run the checker, then reboot and run it again.

39
00:04:21,095 --> 00:04:22,846
Try before you open the solution.

40
00:04:28,366 --> 00:04:33,146
Next up: change passwords and adjust password aging for local user accounts.

41
00:04:33,616 --> 00:04:36,519
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
